  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   This strategy replaces the existing three-phase optimization approach with a novel method centered around \n   periodic candidate generation and a smart covariance matrix optimization. Instead of direct optimization, \n   we simulate periodic signals and apply a covariance and autocorrelation optimization process similar to \n   those used in signal processing for minimax optimization. The central idea is to:\n\n   a) Generate candidate periodic functions using cosine/sine basis decomposition\n   b) Optimize the weights in the convolution such that the autocorrelation peaks are minimized\n   c) Use a covariance matrix adaptation technique to globally scatter search points\n   d) Balance local refinement with global exploration through an adaptive partitioning approach\n\n   The primary expected improvement comes from:\n   - Better handling of multimodality in the search space due to periodic basis\n   - More efficient covariances for large numbers of basins\n   - Reduced dimensionality via periodic constraints\n   - More adaptive neighborhood exploration\n\n   Important limitations:\n   - Requires exact gradient computation for the cost function\n   - Builds integer indexing carefully to match the convolution\n   - Uses adaptive weight distributions that require balancing\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   The optimization takes place over h: [0,2] \u21a6 [0,1] with \u222bh = 1.\n\n   We parameterize h using Fourier bases:\n     h(x) = \u03bc(x)\u00b7w\n   where \u03bc(x) is an expansion basis and w is the coefficient vector.\n\n   The cost function is:\n     C = (1/2) * max_{k\u2208Z} \u0394\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n   For the discrete case:\n     C = (2/240) * max_{k=-511..510} corr[k]  (for n=480)\n\n   Constraints:\n     \u2211h_i * dx = 1\n\n   We use the L2 basis to avoid mixing with the \u201cperiodization\u201d inherent in correlation.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Step 1: Setup\n      a. Discard initial_h_values to enforce algorithm independence\n      b. Use fixed n_points = 240 (optimized balance between computation and resolution)\n      dx = 2/240\n\n   Step 2: Basis Construction\n      Form the n_points \u00d7 n_basis matrix M from pairs of \n        sin(j*\u03c0*x/N) and cos(j*\u03c0*x/N) for j in [0, basis_dim//2)\n      Add a DC bias term for baseline control\n\n   Step 3: Efficient Covariance Setup\n      Initialize covariance matrix \u03a3 with:\n        \u03a3\u2080 = I * 0.6 + 0.2 * ones() * 0.1 * np.abs(1/n_terms)\n      Also set a damping factor for local descent operations\n\n   Step 4: Core Optimization Loop\n      Iterate over steps:\n        - Sample new configurations using Cholesky drawn samples\n        - Apply random perturbation to maintain diversity\n        - Perform local refinement via projected gradient\n        - Use score-driven redistribution of search effort\n\n      Technical Details:\n        * The gradient of the cost is computed explicitly by:\n            \u2202C/\u2202w = (1/(n_lags)) * \u03a3_k \u2202(corr[k])/\u2202w * sign(corr[k] - 2*C)\n        * The covariance is updated using:\n            \u03a3_updated = 0.95 * \u03a3 * (1 + \u03c4 * (y_opt - mean)^2)\n        * Implement efficient \u22651.automated matrix grows\n\n   4. Search Choices and Variations\n      Default configuration:\n        basis_dim = ceil(sqrt(state_dim))   [starting at 35]\n        \u03b1_min = 2.0\n        perturbation_freq = delay^0.75\n        damping = 0.8\n\n      Key adjustments:\n        - Use logarithmic growth for basis dimension\n        - Apply adaptive window for gradient scaling\n        - Inject noise with magnitude proportional to current diversity\n\n   5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n      n_terms up to 47\n      Polynomial time for mixing\n      Wall clock: \n        - Gradient length: O(n_terms^2*L) with L=l \u00b7 d\n        - Projection: O(L^3) minimized via vectorization\n      Check for improvement via early termination: \n        - Gap \u2264 budget_s * 0.9 / 400\n        - Accept if score falls by 0.01% before exceeding CPUs\n\n   6. Validation and Return Contract\n      Ensure:\n        - All indices fall within [0,1] via projection\n        - Integral satisfies: dx * sum(h) = 1\n        - Correlation metrics recomputed exactly\n      Return contract:\n        h_values = h_opt for the given n_points\n        c5_bound = best_computed_C\n        n_points = 240\n\n4. Implementation details\n   Form the basis matrix M (240 x D) where D is an integer multiple of dimensions.\n   The objective function:\n      C_value = (2/n_terms) * max( np.abs( np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full') ) ) * dx\n\n   Iterative steps:\n     while time_remaining > min(100, budget_s/10):\n        global_candidates = sample(N_global) from covariance\n        local_candidates = project(gradients, global_candidates)\n        candidates = deduplicate(local_candidates + global_candidates)\n\n        best_energy = min( compute_candidates(candidates) )\n\n        if improvement > log(improvement_factor) * time_consume:\n           update_covariance(best_energy, gradient)\n\n        fallback to top candidate\n\n5. Supporting Structures\n   Function: compute_gradient(w)\n        h = M @ w   (then clipped to [0,1])\n        corr = np.correlate(h, 1.0-h, mode='full')\n        normalized_corr = corr * dx\n        leading_cols = np.array([ (corr.values > 0.99*C_value) ])\n        grad_contrib = leading_cols * gradient_weights(leading_cols)\n        return grad_contrib @ M\n\n   Function: update_covariance(grad):\n        O = linalg.woodbury_update(covariance, grad)\n        scale(damping\ud83d\udcc0\n        if log||grad|| < min_stop: break\n\n6. Timed Execution\n   Allocate cycles:\n     - Solve for M: fixed\n     - Directions: O(distance measures)\n     - Maintain: O(budget_s//5)\n\n   Run sample configuration with n_terms = 35 for 400s, then scale\n\n7. Fall-Back Validation\n   After each step: recompute and verify integral\n     Norm = 0.0\n     for i in range(len(h)):\n        energy_i = sum(|h[i]|)\n        Grad_i = \u2202C/\u2202h[i] (computed via ) \n        SecondConv_i = energy_i\u00b2\n\n     If (Nullspace cleanup) ...\n\n   All operations vectorized when possible.\n\n8. Integration\n   The optimization harnesses the provided scipy libraries for:\n     - Correlation evaluation\n     - Linear algebra operations\n     - Timer management\n\n   No other external libraries used.\n\n9. Final Check\n   After optimization:\n     h_final = np.clip(h_opt, 0.0, 1.0)\n     verify integral: dx * np.sum(h_final) \u2248 1\n     recompute C5 to match evaluator",
